media.investing.com/news/providers/investing-new.png","title":"Apple Computer Inc receives Investment Bank Analyst Rating Update","updated_at":"2026-09-02T20:03:02Z","commentsCount":0,"category":"","categoryLink":"","pairs":[{"stock_symbol":"AAPL","pair_link":"/equities/apple-computer-inc","pair_name":"Apple Inc","pair_name_synonym":"Apple","pair_change_percent":"-0.06"}],"provider":"Investing.com","date":"2026-09-02 20:03:02","writerId":0,"front_writer_name":"","front_member_name":"","front_writer_link":"","writerName":"","writerLink":"","writerImage":"","userFirstName":"","userLastName":"","writerArticleCount":0,"company_name":"","company_name_prepared":"","company_href":"","company_url":"","user_type":"","authorLink":"","editorId":0,"editorFirstName":"","editorLastName":"","editorLink":"","image_copyright":"\u0026copy; Investing.com","related_image_big":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/1200_7_800x533_L_1783516530.jpg","images_serialized":"a:1:{i:0;a:2:{s:3:\"src\";s:72:\"https://content-media.investing.com/news/1200_7_800x533_L_1783516530.jpg\";s:5:\"width\";i:200;}}","imageHref":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/1200_7_800x533_L_1783516530.jpg","sponsored":false}],"_breakingNewsHeadlines":[],"_featuredNews":[{"article_type":"news","automated_type":null,"body":"Investing.com -- Wall Street on Wednesday rebounded from a slide in the previous session, as surging oil prices and Treasury yields took a breather. A post-earnings jump in Dell Technologies and a rise in Nvidia helped buoy markets, while solid economic data on U.S. factory orders offset a weak read on private employment. \nIt has been a turbulent end to August and start to September for U.S. stocks, as investors remain concerned about inflation and fiscal debt, a resurgence in military strikes between Washington and Tehran, and central bank interest rate hiking.\nThe benchmark S\u0026P 500 added 0.5% to close at 7,667.80 points, while the tech-heavy NASDAQ Composite advanced 0.5% as well to end at 26,217.83 points. The blue-chip Dow Jones Industrial Average climbed 0.6% to settle at 53,061.51 points.\n\"August was a very strong month for stocks, and after this run, investors are now looking to stress test it and figure out what could derail it. While the stock market is currently focused on Iran and the oil price spike, we would ignore this geopolitical and manmade noise and continue to focus attention on the corporate earnings growth picture, which is what ultimately drives stocks higher,\" Michael Landsberg, chief investment officer at Landsberg Bennett Private Wealth Management, said.\n\"The rise in bond yields is a reminder that stocks and bond prices can go down together during environments where rates are rising at a fast pace. Bonds are traditionally thought of as a diversifier for stock volatility, and that can work, but it doesn’t always work. Investors should make sure they have other asset classes that do diversify, such as commodities, in case rates continue to go higher and ultimately take stocks with them,\" he added. \nSoaring AI server demand prompts Dell guidance raise\nDell stock soared almost 16% and ended as the top percentage gainer on the S\u0026P 500, after the company blew past quarterly profit expectations and comfortably beat revenue estimates, helped by soaring demand for its artificial intelligence servers. It also bumped up its full-year top- and bottom-line guidance.\nDell has been one of the biggest winners of the artificial intelligence boom that began in late 2022. The company has partnered with chipmakers to become one of the biggest manufacturers of powerful rack-scale servers and infrastructure capable of powering AI workloads. It has booked tens of billions in AI server orders and built up a massive backlog.\nJeff Clarke, Dell’s chief operating officer, said the AI server business booked an all-time high $60.9 billion in orders, brought in $16.4 billion in revenue, and exited the quarter with a record $95 billion backlog.\nOn the earnings conference call, Clarke said opportunities were extending beyond AI-optimized infrastructure, and demand was broadening across neoclouds, sovereigns, and enterprise customers. \nElsewhere, a 3.2% rise in Nvidia also helped markets. Bloomberg News reported that the AI chipmaking giant was in advance talks to acquire Hugging Face, the dominant open-source AI platform, in a deal valued at approximately $14 billion. Separately, Baird reaffirmed Nvidia as one of its top large-cap ideas, citing the firm’s continued AI compute leadership and growing share in the inference market.\nBond sell-off loses some steam \nA relentless rout in sovereign debt eased up on Wednesday, taking some pressure off equities.\nOn Tuesday, U.S. Treasury yields and other major 10-year instruments across the globe hit multi-year highs. The benchmark U.S. 10-year yield hit its highest level since November 2023, while the 2-year yield hit its highest since July 2024.\nElsewhere, Japan’s 10-year bond yield topped 3% for the first time since August 1996, Germany’s 10-year reached its highest level since March 2011, and the United Kingdom’s 10-year gilt touched its highest since July 2007. \nThe selling has been driven by jitters about inflation amid elevated oil prices, fears about unsustainable debt issuance by companies to fund their AI infrastructure buildouts, and ballooning fiscal debt. Rising yields drive up borrowing costs, which in turn can weigh on consumers and businesses.\nA jump in yields also typically affects growth assets, especially technology stocks. As yields rise, the discount rate increases, automatically shrinking the present value of future earnings. High-duration growth sectors such as software and semiconductors face the heaviest repricing.\n\"One argument is that the rise in bond yields is not bad news, but good news, because it is a logical result of healthy economic growth rates, especially in nominal terms. It may also represent a return to normality after the crazy days of the 2010s and early 2020s, when headline interest rates and benchmark bond yields were near zero. That implied a cost of money, and time, of almost zero, which made little real sense,\" Russ Mould, investment director at AJ Bell, said.\n\"Higher interest rates and bond yields in themselves do not necessarily spell the end of an equity bull run,\" he added.\nThe U.S. Treasury yield curve stabilized on Wednesday, with the 10-year yield last down 1.6 basis points to 4.780%, and the 2-year down 2.1 basis points to 4.373%.\nFactory orders, ADP in focus ahead of Friday’s jobs report\nInterest rate watchers were eyeing more data on the U.S. economy and labor market on Wednesday, ahead of Friday’s closely-watched nonfarm payrolls report for August.\nADP data showed U.S. private employers added 38k jobs in August, lower than the estimated figure of 47k and marking the slowest pace of job creation since January. The update comes a day after U.S. job openings in July came in softer than expected, but ticked up from a downwardly revised figure in June.\n\"The soft ADP report highlights downside risk to the government’s August jobs report, which will be published Friday. Fifth Third Commercial Bank forecasts a below-consensus 25,000 drop in payrolls in that report,\" Bill Adams, chief U.S. economist at the bank, said.\n\"The unemployment rate is forecast to hold steady at 4.1% as the labor force contracts, continuing its recent trend—the labor force is down by 1.3 million workers in the twelve months through July as more older workers retire and fewer graduates and immigrants take their place,\" he added.\nOffsetting ADP was a strong read on U.S. factories. New orders for manufactured good rose 0.9% M/M in July to $663.60 billion, higher than the forecast of an increase of 0.7%. \nAlso on the economic calendar, the Federal Reserve’s latest Beige Book showed regional economic activity increased modestly since early July, with 10 of the 12 Fed districts reporting growth in the slight to moderate range.   \nTrump floats renaming Strait of Hormuz after him \nTurning to the Middle East, oil prices rose, but the advance was not as steep as that on Tuesday. Brent crude futures expiring in November, the global benchmark, were last up 0.4% to $95.04 a barrel, after a 4.6% climb the previous day.\nTensions between the U.S. and Iran have escalated to their highest level since July, amid a resurgence in military strikes between the two. U.S. Central Command on Tuesday said it had completed a new wave of attacks against Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps (IRGC) targets including air defense sites, radar systems, and communications sites. \nIranian state media said the U.S. strikes killed 18 people and injured 108, citing the country’s health minister. State media also said the attacks hit a factory in Qeshm, non-military targets in Hormozgan, and a wedding ceremony in Sirik. Iran retaliated by hitting Camp Titin, a U.S. Marine base in Jordan, with ballistic missiles, state media added. \nA statement from the Iranian foreign ministry condemned the U.S. strikes, calling them \"terrorist and sabotage\" attacks and labeling them as \"war crimes.\"\nWashington and Tehran had been locked in an impasse over control of the Strait of Hormuz coming into last weekend, and President Donald Trump had appeared to shift his strategy from military strikes to economic warfare. However, kinetic action resumed on Sunday for the first time since July.\n\"Now that we have it under U.S.A. control, should we change the name Hormuz Strait to TRUMP STRAIT???\" the president posted on his Truth Social service on Wednesday.\nAmbar Warrick and Pranav Kashyap contributed reporting","category_ids":[25],"country_ids":[5],"created_by":275864531,"domain_id":1,"economic_event_ids":null,"edited_by":0,"editors_pick":false,"featured":true,"id":4885211,"important":false,"instruments":[{"id":20,"primary_tag":false},{"id":166,"primary_tag":false},{"id":169,"primary_tag":false},{"id":8849,"primary_tag":false},{"id":8906,"primary_tag":false},{"id":1187654,"primary_tag":false},{"id":1187655,"primary_tag":false}],"keywords":"","link":"/news/stock-market-news/us-futures-muted-as-markets-parse-rate-jitters-more-iran-strikes-4885211","media":[{"caption":"","copyright":"\u0026copy; Reuters","purpose":"preview_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/LYNXMPEM4C1QN_150x108_S.jpg"},{"caption":"","copyright":"\u0026copy; Reuters","purpose":"medium_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/LYNXMPEM4C1QN_M.jpg"},{"caption":"","copyright":"\u0026copy; Reuters","purpose":"main_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/LYNXMPEM4C1QN_1200_L.jpg"},{"caption":"","copyright":"","purpose":"article_source_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/providers/investing-new.png"}],"metadata":{},"multi_publish_headers":null,"news_type":"AkapiAdmin","newsType":"AkapiAdmin","publish_company_id":null,"published_at":"2026-09-02T00:14:54Z","source_external_link":"","source_id":10,"source_name":"Investing.com","source_image":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/providers/investing-new.png","title":"Wall Street ends higher on Dell, Nvidia boost and a let up in oil prices, yields","updated_at":"2026-09-02T20:09:18Z","commentsCount":121,"category":"","categoryLink":"","provider":"Investing.com","date":"2026-09-02 20:09:18","writerId":0,"front_writer_name":"","front_member_name":"","front_writer_link":"","writerName":"","writerLink":"","writerImage":"","userFirstName":"","userLastName":"","writerArticleCount":0,"company_name":"","company_name_prepared":"","company_href":"","company_url":"","user_type":"","authorLink":"","editorId":0,"editorFirstName":"","editorLastName":"","editorLink":"","image_copyright":"\u0026copy; Reuters","related_image_big":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/LYNXMPEM4C1QN_1200_L.jpg","images_serialized":"a:1:{i:0;a:2:{s:3:\"src\";s:65:\"https://content-media.investing.com/news/LYNXMPEM4C1QN_1200_L.jpg\";s:5:\"width\";i:200;}}","imageHref":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/LYNXMPEM4C1QN_1200_L.jpg","sponsored":false},{"article_type":"news","automated_type":null,"body":"Investing.com-- Oil prices edged slightly lower on Wednesday, steadying after logging strong gains driven by renewed fighting between the United States and Iran, which heightened fears of prolonged disruptions to crude supplies from the Middle East.\nAs of 07:32 ET (11:32 GMT), Brent crude futures for November slipped 0.2% to $94.48 a barrel, while West Texas Intermediate (WTI) crude futures fell 0.8% to $89.49 a barrel, according to Investing.com data. Brent earlier climbed as high as $97.04 a barrel.\nBoth benchmarks jumped nearly 5% in the previous session and reached their highest levels in about five weeks.\n\nThe U.S. launched a fresh barrage of airstrikes against Iranian targets overnight, prompting retaliatory missile and drone attacks by Tehran against U.S. forces in Jordan and Bahrain.\nThe escalation was among the most serious exchanges between the two countries in weeks.\nThe renewed confrontation has raised concern that the conflict could further restrict tanker movements through the Strait of Hormuz and deepen an already significant supply shock. \nThe risks intensified after two supertankers carrying Saudi crude were hit by unidentified projectiles while passing through the strait on Monday. The vessels had each loaded about 2 million barrels at Saudi Arabia’s Juaymah terminal.\n\"We’ve seen oil flow through the Strait of Hormuz despite the stalemate between the US and Iran, but rising tensions clearly put crossings at risk,\" ING analysts said in a note.\nThe U.S. energy secretary said 17 million barrels of oil flowed through the Strait of Hormuz on Monday, but analysts said that ship-tracking data suggest more modest volumes, making longer-term averages a more reliable measure of flows.\n\"Given disruptions to Middle East and Russian diesel exports, and with little sign of an imminent recovery, middle distillate cracks are likely to remain highly elevated and volatile, particularly as we move towards seasonally stronger demand,\" ING analysts added.\nIranian crude exports have also been severely curtailed. Reuters reported that Iranian crude loadings fell to between 220,000 and 255,000 barrels per day in August from about 2 million bpd in March.\nMeanwhile, U.S. crude oil inventories fell by 2.6 million barrels in the week ended Aug. 28, according to data from the American Petroleum Institute released late Tuesday, reversing a 4.2 million-barrel increase in the previous week. \nGasoline inventories rose by about 300,000 barrels, while distillate stocks fell by roughly 300,000 barrels, according to the API data. \nThe API data preceded the more closely watched weekly report from the U.S. Energy Information Administration, due later Wednesday.\nVahid Karaahmetovic contributed to this report. ","category_ids":[11],"country_ids":[4,5],"created_by":264090824,"domain_id":1,"economic_event_ids":null,"edited_by":0,"editors_pick":false,"featured":true,"id":4885235,"important":false,"instruments":[{"id":8833,"primary_tag":true},{"id":8849,"primary_tag":true}],"keywords":"","link":"/news/commodities-news/oil-climbs-for-3rd-straight-day-brent-tops-96-on-renewed-usiran-fighting-4885235","media":[{"caption":"","copyright":"\u0026copy; Reuters","purpose":"preview_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/moved_small-LYNXMPEM6Q18J_1200_L.jpg"},{"caption":"","copyright":"\u0026copy; Reuters","purpose":"main_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/moved_LYNXMPEM6Q18J_1200_L.jpg"},{"caption":"","copyright":"","purpose":"article_source_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/providers/investing-new.png"}],"metadata":{},"multi_publish_headers":null,"news_type":"AkapiAdmin","newsType":"AkapiAdmin","publish_company_id":null,"published_at":"2026-09-02T01:03:24Z","source_external_link":"","source_id":10,"source_name":"Investing.com","source_image":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/providers/investing-new.png","title":"Oil prices edge lower after recent gains on renewed U.S.-Iran tensions","updated_at":"2026-09-02T11:36:20Z","commentsCount":18,"category":"","categoryLink":"","provider":"Investing.com","date":"2026-09-02 11:36:20","writerId":0,"front_writer_name":"","front_member_name":"","front_writer_link":"","writerName":"","writerLink":"","writerImage":"","userFirstName":"","userLastName":"","writerArticleCount":0,"company_name":"","company_name_prepared":"","company_href":"","company_url":"","user_type":"","authorLink":"","editorId":0,"editorFirstName":"","editorLastName":"","editorLink":"","image_copyright":"\u0026copy; Reuters","related_image_big":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/moved_LYNXMPEM6Q18J_1200_L.jpg","images_serialized":"a:1:{i:0;a:2:{s:3:\"src\";s:71:\"https://content-media.investing.com/news/moved_LYNXMPEM6Q18J_1200_L.jpg\";s:5:\"width\";i:200;}}","imageHref":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/moved_LYNXMPEM6Q18J_1200_L.jpg","sponsored":false},{"article_type":"news","automated_type":null,"body":"Investing.com-- The U.S. and Iran exchanged strikes on Tuesday, marking their worst military tensions since late-July amid continued clashes over the Strait of Hormuz \nCENTCOM said in an X post it had concluded a wave of strikes against Iran’s Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps targets by Tuesday evening, having targeted air defense sites, radar systems, and naval assets.  \nCENTCOM said the strikes followed recent IRGC attacks on commercial shipping in the Strait of Hormuz and against American service members in the region.\n\nIranian media reports said the country retaliated by attacking multiple U.S.-linked targets in Iraq, Jordan, and Bahrain. The country was also seen threatening commercial vessels in and around Hormuz. \nThe latest exchange marks the worst U.S.-Iran fighting seen since late-July, as the two remained at loggerheads over the Strait of Hormuz. \nWashington continued to claim that Hormuz remained open, while Iran asserted that the crossing was closed for all commercial traffic. \nU.S. President Donald Trump in an interview on Tuesday vowed more action against Iran, threatening to “hit them hard.” Separately, Trump also threatened to attack Iran’s Kharg Island, a major oil export hub for the country. \n“I ‌like our position ⁠now much better, with almost total control of the Hormuz Strait, and their economy totally collapsing… They are just playing out the inevitable,” Trump said in a social media post on Tuesday evening. \nIran threatened the U.S.’ Gulf allies, warning them to expel U.S. forces from their territories or face even more Iranian aggression. \nOil prices surged this week, as the fighting indicated little progress towards a U.S.-Iran peace deal. Supply through Hormuz also remained constrained, with shipping data showing traffic in the waterway remained at a fraction of prewar levels. \n \n \n ","category_ids":[14],"country_ids":[4],"created_by":240569828,"domain_id":1,"economic_event_ids":null,"edited_by":0,"editors_pick":false,"featured":true,"id":4885300,"important":false,"instruments":[{"id":8833,"primary_tag":true}],"keywords":"","link":"/news/economy-news/us-iran-trade-more-strikes-as-hormuz-tensions-simmer-4885300","media":[{"caption":"","copyright":"\u0026copy; Reuters. ","purpose":"preview_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/LYNXNPEE1R1IN_S.jpg"},{"caption":"","copyright":"\u0026copy; Reuters. ","purpose":"medium_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/LYNXNPEE1R1IN_M.jpg"},{"caption":"","copyright":"\u0026copy; Reuters. ","purpose":"main_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/LYNXNPEE1R1IN_L.jpg"},{"caption":"","copyright":"","purpose":"article_source_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/providers/investing-new.png"}],"metadata":{},"multi_publish_headers":null,"news_type":"AkapiAdmin","newsType":"AkapiAdmin","publish_company_id":null,"published_at":"2026-09-02T04:06:48Z","source_external_link":"","source_id":10,"source_name":"Investing.com","source_image":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/providers/investing-new.png","title":"U.S., Iran trade more strikes as Hormuz tensions simmer","updated_at":"2026-09-02T04:06:48Z","commentsCount":20,"category":"","categoryLink":"","provider":"Investing.com","date":"2026-09-02 04:06:48","writerId":0,"front_writer_name":"","front_member_name":"","front_writer_link":"","writerName":"","writerLink":"","writerImage":"","userFirstName":"","userLastName":"","writerArticleCount":0,"company_name":"","company_name_prepared":"","company_href":"","company_url":"","user_type":"","authorLink":"","editorId":0,"editorFirstName":"","editorLastName":"","editorLink":"","image_copyright":"\u0026copy; Reuters.","related_image_big":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/LYNXNPEE1R1IN_L.jpg","images_serialized":"a:1:{i:0;a:2:{s:3:\"src\";s:60:\"https://content-media.investing.com/news/LYNXNPEE1R1IN_L.jpg\";s:5:\"width\";i:200;}}","imageHref":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/LYNXNPEE1R1IN_L.jpg","sponsored":false},{"article_type":"news","automated_type":null,"body":"","category_ids":[461],"country_ids":[5],"created_by":256370160,"domain_id":1,"economic_event_ids":null,"edited_by":null,"editors_pick":false,"featured":true,"id":4886604,"important":true,"instruments":[{"id":8358,"primary_tag":true}],"keywords":"","link":"/news/pro/this-beverage-giants-international-growth-cant-hide-ongoing-us-weakness-bofa-432SI-4886604","media":[{"caption":"","copyright":"\u0026copy; Reuters. ","purpose":"preview_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/LYNXMPEA6T034_S.jpg"},{"caption":"","copyright":"\u0026copy; Reuters. ","purpose":"medium_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/LYNXMPEA6T034_M.jpg"},{"caption":"","copyright":"\u0026copy; Reuters. ","purpose":"main_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/LYNXMPEA6T034_L.jpg"},{"caption":"","copyright":"","purpose":"article_source_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/providers/investing-new.png"}],"metadata":{},"multi_publish_headers":null,"news_type":"InvestingPro","newsType":"InvestingPro","publish_company_id":null,"published_at":"2026-09-02T18:00:00Z","source_external_link":"https://www.streetinsider.com/","source_id":80,"source_name":"Investing.com","source_image":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/providers/investing-new.png","title":"This beverage giant’s international growth can’t hide ongoing U.S. weakness: BofA","updated_at":"2026-09-02T18:01:02Z","commentsCount":0,"category":"","categoryLink":"","provider":"Investing.com","date":"2026-09-02 18:01:02","writerId":0,"front_writer_name":"","front_member_name":"","front_writer_link":"","writerName":"","writerLink":"","writerImage":"","userFirstName":"","userLastName":"","writerArticleCount":0,"company_name":"","company_name_prepared":"","company_href":"","company_url":"","user_type":"","authorLink":"","editorId":0,"editorFirstName":"","editorLastName":"","editorLink":"","image_copyright":"\u0026copy; Reuters.","related_image_big":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/LYNXMPEA6T034_L.jpg","images_serialized":"a:1:{i:0;a:2:{s:3:\"src\";s:60:\"https://content-media.investing.com/news/LYNXMPEA6T034_L.jpg\";s:5:\"width\";i:200;}}","imageHref":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/LYNXMPEA6T034_L.jpg","sponsored":false},{"article_type":"news","automated_type":null,"body":"Investing.com -- Dell Technologies on Tuesday blew past quarterly profit expectations and comfortably beat revenue forecasts, helped by soaring demand for its artificial intelligence servers. It also bumped up its full-year top- and bottom-line guidance. \nShares of the company advanced more than 10% in early trading Wednesday by 09:31 ET (13:31 GMT). \nDell has been one of the biggest winners of the artificial intelligence boom that began in late 2022. The company has partnered with chipmakers like Nvidia to become one of the biggest manufacturers of powerful rack-scale servers and infrastructure capable of powering AI workloads. It has booked tens of billions in AI server orders and built up a massive backlog.\nJeff Clarke, DELL’s chief operating officer, said the AI server business booked an all-time high $60.9 billion in orders, brought in $16.4 billion in revenue, and exited the quarter with a record $95 billion backlog.\nRevenue from Dell’s Infrastructure Solutions Group (ISG) segment, which houses the AI server business, has now become roughly double of revenue from Dell’s legacy hardware division, or the Client Solutions Group (CSG), covering its personal computers, laptops, tablets, and other such products.\nISG revenue in the quarter surged 89% Y/Y to $31.78 billion, while CSG revenue climbed 20% to $15.03 billion. \nThe Round Rock, Texas-based company earned $7.04 per share on an adjusted basis on revenue of $46.97 billion for its fiscal Q2 2027. Analysts had expected adjusted profit of $4.87 per share on revenue of $44.84 billion.\nLooking at guidance, Dell sees fiscal Q3 2027 adjusted earnings per share of $6.50 on revenue of $49 billion, versus an anticipated $41.91 billion. \nFor the full fiscal year 2027, the firm sees adjusted earnings per share of $25.50 on revenue of $192 billion, versus a prior outlook of $17.90 per share on revenue of $167 billion. The top-line consensus estimate was $173.8 billion. \nRaymond James analyst Simon Leopold reiterated the Outperform rating on Dell after the report and raised the price target to $617 from $500, highlighting \"a solid F2Q27 beat and a significantly higher F3Q27 and FY27 outlook.\"\n\"Although pricing and pull-forward orders are likely factors to some degree, the revised outlook implies AI is pulling through demand across traditional compute and storage, and the margin expansion this quarter also demonstrates Dell’s ability to execute despite continued supply constraints,\" he wrote. \nVahid Karaahmetovic contributed to this report. ","category_ids":[1062],"country_ids":[5],"created_by":275864531,"domain_id":1,"economic_event_ids":null,"edited_by":0,"editors_pick":false,"featured":true,"id":4884587,"important":false,"instruments":[{"id":257,"primary_tag":true}],"keywords":"","link":"/news/earnings/dell-stock-soars-10-after-hours-on-quarterly-results-raised-full-year-guidance-4884587","media":[{"caption":"","copyright":"\u0026copy; Reuters. ","purpose":"preview_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/LYNXNPEA8L02Z_S.jpg"},{"caption":"","copyright":"\u0026copy; Reuters. ","purpose":"medium_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/LYNXNPEA8L02Z_M.jpg"},{"caption":"","copyright":"\u0026copy; Reuters. ","purpose":"main_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/LYNXNPEA8L02Z_L.jpg"},{"caption":"","copyright":"","purpose":"article_source_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/providers/investing-new.png"}],"metadata":{},"multi_publish_headers":null,"news_type":"AkapiAdmin","newsType":"AkapiAdmin","publish_company_id":null,"published_at":"2026-09-01T20:27:53Z","source_external_link":"","source_id":10,"source_name":"Investing.com","source_image":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/providers/investing-new.png","title":"Dell stock rallies 10% as soaring AI server demand prompts guidance raise","updated_at":"2026-09-02T13:31:48Z","commentsCount":3,"category":"","categoryLink":"","provider":"Investing.com","date":"2026-09-02 13:31:48","writerId":0,"front_writer_name":"","front_member_name":"","front_writer_link":"","writerName":"","writerLink":"","writerImage":"","userFirstName":"","userLastName":"","writerArticleCount":0,"company_name":"","company_name_prepared":"","company_href":"","company_url":"","user_type":"","authorLink":"","editorId":0,"editorFirstName":"","editorLastName":"","editorLink":"","image_copyright":"\u0026copy; Reuters.","related_image_big":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/LYNXNPEA8L02Z_L.jpg","images_serialized":"a:1:{i:0;a:2:{s:3:\"src\";s:60:\"https://content-media.investing.com/news/LYNXNPEA8L02Z_L.jpg\";s:5:\"width\";i:200;}}","imageHref":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/LYNXNPEA8L02Z_L.jpg","sponsored":false},{"article_type":"news","automated_type":null,"body":"Investing.com -- The Bank of Canada kept its policy rate unchanged on Wednesday as a strengthening domestic economy was weighed against rising inflation risks from elevated oil prices and growing uncertainty over trade with the United States.\nThe central bank held its overnight rate target at 2.25%, with the Bank Rate at 2.50% and the deposit rate at 2.20%. The decision was in line with its July outlook, but policymakers warned that risks to inflation had increased while new U.S. tariffs had made the growth outlook more uncertain.\n\nCanada’s economy rebounded strongly in the second quarter after a weak start to the year, with the recovery broadening across consumption, housing, exports and business investment. The unemployment rate also edged lower in July, although the Bank said labour demand remained subdued and the economy continued to show excess capacity.\nThe Bank said inflation had hovered around 3% in recent months, driven largely by persistently higher gasoline prices. Excluding gasoline, inflation stood at 2.2% in July, while its preferred measures of core inflation remained close to 2%.\n\u0026ldquo;With the Middle East conflict still ongoing and little progress reopening the Strait of Hormuz, upside risks to the Bank\u0026rsquo;s inflation forecast have increased,\u0026rdquo; the Bank said.\nThis is a developing story, please check back for updates. ","category_ids":[14],"country_ids":[4],"created_by":278602837,"domain_id":1,"economic_event_ids":null,"edited_by":null,"editors_pick":false,"featured":true,"id":4886338,"important":false,"instruments":[{"id":8833,"primary_tag":true}],"keywords":"","link":"/news/economy-news/bank-of-canada-holds-rate-as-tariffs-oil-prices-cloud-outlook-4886338","media":[{"caption":"","copyright":"\u0026copy; Reuters","purpose":"preview_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/BankofCanada_150x108_S_1601499520.jpg"},{"caption":"","copyright":"\u0026copy; Reuters","purpose":"main_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/BankofCanada_800x533_L_1601499520.jpg"},{"caption":"","copyright":"\u0026copy; Reuters","purpose":"medium_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/BankofCanada_800x533_L_1601499520.jpg"},{"caption":"","copyright":"","purpose":"article_source_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/providers/investing-new.png"}],"metadata":{},"multi_publish_headers":null,"news_type":"AkapiAdmin","newsType":"AkapiAdmin","publish_company_id":null,"published_at":"2026-09-02T13:56:08Z","source_external_link":"","source_id":10,"source_name":"Investing.com","source_image":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/providers/investing-new.png","title":"Bank of Canada holds rate as tariffs, oil prices cloud outlook","updated_at":"2026-09-02T13:56:08Z","commentsCount":0,"category":"","categoryLink":"","provider":"Investing.com","date":"2026-09-02 13:56:08","writerId":0,"front_writer_name":"","front_member_name":"","front_writer_link":"","writerName":"","writerLink":"","writerImage":"","userFirstName":"","userLastName":"","writerArticleCount":0,"company_name":"","company_name_prepared":"","company_href":"","company_url":"","user_type":"","authorLink":"","editorId":0,"editorFirstName":"","editorLastName":"","editorLink":"","image_copyright":"\u0026copy; Reuters","related_image_big":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/BankofCanada_800x533_L_1601499520.jpg","images_serialized":"a:1:{i:0;a:2:{s:3:\"src\";s:78:\"https://content-media.investing.com/news/BankofCanada_800x533_L_1601499520.jpg\";s:5:\"width\";i:200;}}","imageHref":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/BankofCanada_800x533_L_1601499520.jpg","sponsored":false},{"article_type":"news","automated_type":null,"body":"Investing.com -- Gold prices extended their losses on Wednesday, staying near a more than three-week low as a stronger dollar and rising Treasury yields reinforced expectations for a Federal Reserve rate hike.\nRenewed U.S.-Iran tensions have also pushed oil prices higher, adding to inflation concerns.\nAt 07:42 ET (11:42 GMT), XAU/USD traded flat at $4,327.56 an ounce, while Gold Futures declined 0.5% to $4,373.00. XAG/USD rose 0.2% to $64.22 an ounce, while XPT/USD declined 0.5% to $1,736.90. The US Dollar Index gained 0.1% to 99.73.\n\nIran escalation lifts oil and Fed hike expectations\nGold has now fallen for a fourth straight session, extending its retreat from last week’s high near $4,700. The metal has come under pressure as rising oil prices, higher bond yields and a firmer dollar reinforce expectations that the Fed may need to keep rates higher for longer.\nThe latest pressure followed a fresh round of U.S. strikes against targets in Iran on Tuesday. Tehran said it retaliated, marking a sharp escalation after nearly a month of relative calm.\nBrent crude edged lower to $94 a barrel and U.S. crude slipped below $90, but remained near recent highs as traders assessed the risk that a prolonged conflict could disrupt energy flows through the Strait of Hormuz. \nHigher oil prices matter for gold because energy costs can feed directly into inflation. That can make the Federal Reserve increase the likelihood of further tightening. Markets are now pricing close to a 70% probability of a Fed rate hike at the September 15 to 16 meeting.\nThe shift follows Federal Reserve Chair Kevin Warsh’s hawkish Jackson Hole message last week and fresh warnings from Fed officials that inflation could remain entrenched.\nFed Governor Michael Barr said on Tuesday that policymakers should be prepared to raise interest rates if inflation does not ease, warning that price pressures could become embedded after remaining above the Fed’s target for more than five years.\nGlobal bond selloff undermines Treasury rally\nThe inflation shock is also playing out across global bond markets. Long dated U.S. Treasury yields have climbed back to levels seen before the Treasury’s surprise intervention last month, erasing much of the initial impact of the move.\nThirty year Treasury yields climbed above 5.28% on Tuesday, returning to the level seen before Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent announced the buyback expansion on August 19.\nThe broader move has also pushed government bond yields higher across major markets, with global yields reaching their highest levels since 2008. The dollar has strengthened alongside the selloff, creating another headwind because a stronger greenback makes dollar priced gold more expensive for international buyers.\nGold’s recent decline follows a nearly 10% gain in August, its strongest monthly performance since January. That rally accelerated after the Treasury increased bond buybacks and revived the debasement trade, which reflects concerns about rising sovereign debt and potential currency depreciation.\nANZ said the Treasury’s liquidity intervention had initially encouraged investors to increase their gold exposure. The latest reversal in yields and the dollar has now curtailed that momentum, although ANZ expects the broader debasement theme to continue attracting buyers.\nTechnically, gold has also suffered further damage after breaking below its 200 day moving average, a level widely watched as a measure of longer term momentum.\nVahid Karaahmetovic contributed to this report. ","category_ids":[11],"country_ids":[4,5,242,999],"created_by":281410296,"domain_id":1,"economic_event_ids":null,"edited_by":0,"editors_pick":false,"featured":true,"id":4885255,"important":false,"instruments":[{"id":68,"primary_tag":false},{"id":8830,"primary_tag":true},{"id":8833,"primary_tag":true},{"id":8849,"primary_tag":true},{"id":23706,"primary_tag":true},{"id":942611,"primary_tag":true}],"keywords":"","link":"/news/commodities-news/gold-falls-as-oil-bond-yields-and-fed-hike-bets-climb-on-iran-escalation-4885255","media":[{"caption":"","copyright":"\u0026copy; Reuters. ","purpose":"preview_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/LYNXMPED180L9_S.jpg"},{"caption":"","copyright":"\u0026copy; Reuters. ","purpose":"medium_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/LYNXMPED180L9_M.jpg"},{"caption":"","copyright":"\u0026copy; Reuters. ","purpose":"main_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/LYNXMPED180L9_L.jpg"},{"caption":"","copyright":"","purpose":"article_source_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/providers/investing-new.png"}],"metadata":{},"multi_publish_headers":null,"news_type":"AkapiAdmin","newsType":"AkapiAdmin","publish_company_id":null,"published_at":"2026-09-02T01:57:59Z","source_external_link":"","source_id":10,"source_name":"Investing.com","source_image":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/providers/investing-new.png","title":"Gold extends losses to 3-week low as stronger dollar, Fed hike bets weigh","updated_at":"2026-09-02T11:45:47Z","commentsCount":3,"category":"","categoryLink":"","provider":"Investing.com","date":"2026-09-02 11:45:47","writerId":0,"front_writer_name":"","front_member_name":"","front_writer_link":"","writerName":"","writerLink":"","writerImage":"","userFirstName":"","userLastName":"","writerArticleCount":0,"company_name":"","company_name_prepared":"","company_href":"","company_url":"","user_type":"","authorLink":"","editorId":0,"editorFirstName":"","editorLastName":"","editorLink":"","image_copyright":"\u0026copy; Reuters.","related_image_big":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/LYNXMPED180L9_L.jpg","images_serialized":"a:1:{i:0;a:2:{s:3:\"src\";s:60:\"https://content-media.investing.com/news/LYNXMPED180L9_L.jpg\";s:5:\"width\";i:200;}}","imageHref":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/LYNXMPED180L9_L.jpg","sponsored":false},{"article_type":"news","automated_type":null,"body":"","category_ids":[461],"country_ids":[26],"created_by":261166919,"domain_id":1,"economic_event_ids":null,"edited_by":null,"editors_pick":false,"featured":true,"id":4885327,"important":true,"instruments":[{"id":14615,"primary_tag":true}],"keywords":"","link":"/news/pro/jp-morgan-says-this-spanish-travel-stock-could-benefit-from-ai-despite-disruption-risks-432SI-4885327","media":[{"caption":"","copyright":"\u0026copy; Investing.com","purpose":"preview_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/1200_3_150x108_S_1783516353.jpg"},{"caption":"","copyright":"\u0026copy; Investing.com","purpose":"medium_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/1200_3_800x533_L_1783516353.jpg"},{"caption":"","copyright":"\u0026copy; Investing.com","purpose":"main_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/1200_3_800x533_L_1783516353.jpg"},{"caption":"","copyright":"","purpose":"article_source_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/providers/investing-new.png"}],"metadata":{},"multi_publish_headers":null,"news_type":"InvestingPro","newsType":"InvestingPro","publish_company_id":null,"published_at":"2026-09-02T06:00:00Z","source_external_link":"https://www.streetinsider.com/","source_id":80,"source_name":"Investing.com","source_image":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/providers/investing-new.png","title":"J.P. Morgan says this Spanish travel stock could benefit from AI despite disruption risks","updated_at":"2026-09-02T06:02:02Z","commentsCount":0,"category":"","categoryLink":"","provider":"Investing.com","date":"2026-09-02 06:02:02","writerId":0,"front_writer_name":"","front_member_name":"","front_writer_link":"","writerName":"","writerLink":"","writerImage":"","userFirstName":"","userLastName":"","writerArticleCount":0,"company_name":"","company_name_prepared":"","company_href":"","company_url":"","user_type":"","authorLink":"","editorId":0,"editorFirstName":"","editorLastName":"","editorLink":"","image_copyright":"\u0026copy; Investing.com","related_image_big":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/1200_3_800x533_L_1783516353.jpg","images_serialized":"a:1:{i:0;a:2:{s:3:\"src\";s:72:\"https://content-media.investing.com/news/1200_3_800x533_L_1783516353.jpg\";s:5:\"width\";i:200;}}","imageHref":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/1200_3_800x533_L_1783516353.jpg","sponsored":false},{"article_type":"news","automated_type":null,"body":"Investing.com -- A federal judge rejected the Justice Department’s request to force Alphabet Inc.’s Google to sell its advertising exchange on Wednesday, sparing the tech company from a second breakup attempt.\nJudge Leonie Brinkema issued her decision under seal with a brief order that denied the Justice Department’s demand to force a sale of Google’s AdX. The judge instead ordered behavioral changes to Google’s business operations, though the specific requirements were not disclosed. A redacted version of the decision will be released at a later date.\n\nThe ruling allows Google to keep its advertising exchange following an April 2025 decision that found the company illegally monopolized two advertising technology markets. The Justice Department had sought to force Google to sell its exchange and make public the auction logic that determines which advertisements appear on websites.\nThe ad exchange represents a small portion of Google’s overall business. Publishers pay Google a 20% fee to sell ads in auctions that occur instantly when users load websites.\nGoogle shares traded roughly 1% higher on Wednesday.\nThe ruling marks the second time Google has successfully defended against Justice Department efforts to force asset sales to address illegal monopolies.","category_ids":[25],"country_ids":[5],"created_by":235660125,"domain_id":1,"economic_event_ids":null,"edited_by":0,"editors_pick":false,"featured":true,"id":4886459,"important":false,"instruments":[{"id":6369,"primary_tag":true}],"keywords":"","link":"/news/stock-market-news/google-avoids-breakup-as-judge-rejects-ad-tech-sale-order-4886459","media":[{"caption":"","copyright":"\u0026copy; Reuters","purpose":"preview_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/moved_small-LYNXMPEL6E0E1_L.jpg"},{"caption":"","copyright":"\u0026copy; Reuters","purpose":"main_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/moved_LYNXMPEL6E0E1_L.jpg"},{"caption":"","copyright":"","purpose":"article_source_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/providers/investing-new.png"}],"metadata":{},"multi_publish_headers":null,"news_type":"AkapiAdmin","newsType":"AkapiAdmin","publish_company_id":null,"published_at":"2026-09-02T14:39:59Z","source_external_link":"","source_id":10,"source_name":"Investing.com","source_image":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/providers/investing-new.png","title":"Google avoids breakup as judge rejects ad tech sale order","updated_at":"2026-09-02T14:39:59Z","commentsCount":0,"category":"","categoryLink":"","provider":"Investing.com","date":"2026-09-02 14:39:59","writerId":0,"front_writer_name":"","front_member_name":"","front_writer_link":"","writerName":"","writerLink":"","writerImage":"","userFirstName":"","userLastName":"","writerArticleCount":0,"company_name":"","company_name_prepared":"","company_href":"","company_url":"","user_type":"","authorLink":"","editorId":0,"editorFirstName":"","editorLastName":"","editorLink":"","image_copyright":"\u0026copy; Reuters","related_image_big":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/moved_LYNXMPEL6E0E1_L.jpg","images_serialized":"a:1:{i:0;a:2:{s:3:\"src\";s:66:\"https://content-media.investing.com/news/moved_LYNXMPEL6E0E1_L.jpg\";s:5:\"width\";i:200;}}","imageHref":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/moved_LYNXMPEL6E0E1_L.jpg","sponsored":false},{"article_type":"news","automated_type":null,"body":"Investing.com -- The U.S. dollar on Wednesday inched lower after catching a safe haven bid in the previous session, amid a let up in surging oil prices and Treasury yields. The loonie and the kiwi grabbed some attention after interest rate decisions in the respective countries, while the Japanese yen strengthened on intervention hopes. \nAt 15:59 ET (19:59 GMT), the U.S. dollar index, which tracks the greenback against a basket of six major peers, was down 0.1% to 99.60. The gauge had climbed 0.3% on Tuesday. \nOil, yields take a breather\nA relentless rout in sovereign debt eased up on Wednesday, prompting traders to move back into equities. \nOn Tuesday, U.S. Treasury yields and other major 10-year instruments across the globe hit multi-year highs. The benchmark U.S. 10-year yield hit its highest level since November 2023, while the 2-year yield hit its highest since July 2024.\nThe selling has been driven by jitters about inflation amid elevated oil prices, fears about unsustainable debt issuance by companies to fund their AI infrastructure buildouts, and ballooning fiscal debt. Rising yields drive up borrowing costs, which in turn can weigh on consumers and businesses. Higher rate environments also tend to boost the dollar.\nThe U.S. Treasury yield curve stabilized on Wednesday, with the 10-year yield last down 1.2 basis point to 4.784%, and the 2-year down 2.1 basis points to 4.373%.\nInterest rate watchers also received data on the U.S. economy and labor market, ahead of Friday’s closely-watched nonfarm payrolls report for August.\nADP data showed U.S. private employers added 38k jobs in August, lower than the estimated figure of 47k and marking the slowest pace of job creation since January. Offsetting ADP was a strong read on U.S. factories. New orders for manufactured good rose 0.9% M/M in July to $663.60 billion, higher than the forecast of an increase of 0.7%.\nAlso on the economic calendar, the Federal Reserve’s latest Beige Book showed regional economic activity increased modestly since early July, with 10 of the 12 Fed districts reporting growth in the slight to moderate range.\nElsewhere, oil prices rose, but the advance was not as steep as that on Tuesday. Brent crude futures expiring in November, the global benchmark, were last up 0.6% to $95.26 a barrel, after a 4.6% climb the previous day.\nTensions between the U.S. and Iran have escalated to their highest level in weeks, amid a resurgence in military strikes between the two. U.S. Central Command on Tuesday said it had completed a new wave of attacks against Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps (IRGC) targets including air defense sites, radar systems, and communications sites.\nWashington and Tehran had been locked in an impasse over control of the Strait of Hormuz coming into last weekend, and President Donald Trump had appeared to shift his strategy from military strikes to economic warfare. However, kinetic action resumed on Sunday for the first time since July.\nLoonie perks up after BoC holds pat\nTurning to other major currencies, the Canadian dollar gained 0.4% on Wednesday. Earlier, the Bank of Canada (BoC) held its key overnight rate steady, but warned that risks to inflation had increased and a trade war with the U.S. had made the growth outlook more uncertain.\nBoC Governor Tiff Macklem noted that since the last rate decision in July, the Middle East conflict appeared to be no closer to a resolution. He also highlighted that new U.S. tariffs could pose risks to a rebound in growth, though he didn’t expect the levies to have a large direct impact on the overall level of economic activity.\n\"Governor Macklem appeared to downplay the growth impacts from the trade war escalation, suggesting that the direct impacts from the tariffs would be narrow and that businesses are adapting to tariffs and uncertainty. He expressed several times greater concern about upside risks to inflation emanating from the conflict in the Middle East,\" David Doyle, head of economics at Macquarie, said.\n\"We continue to see rate hikes ahead with the first 25 bps hike in December, a view we updated recently due to the escalation in trade tensions with the U.S. Should trade tensions subside in coming weeks (and uncertainty diminish) an earlier hike in October may occur. In total we now expect 125 bps of hikes ahead from the BoC (prev. 75 bps) through end-27 which would bring the Overnight rate to 3.5%,\" he added.\nKiwi suffers worst daily drop since March on RBNZ pivot\nThe New Zealand dollar was another notable mover, falling over 0.7% against the greenback and on track for its worst day since June 23.\nThe move came after the Reserve Bank of New Zealand’s (RBNZ) monetary policy decision, where policymakers delivered a 25-basis-point rate hike but paired the move with unexpectedly dovish forward guidance.\nRBNZ officials adopted deliberate ambiguity regarding future tightening, signaling that an October rate increase is far from guaranteed and emphasizing data-dependent flexibility.\nYen pulls back from 160 threshold as BoJ prepares rate debate\nIn Asia, the Japanese yen strengthened against the dollar and pulled back from the key 160 level. Speaking at a news conference earlier, Bank of Japan (BoJ) Governor Kazuo Ueda confirmed that a rate hike would be debated in September, focusing on whether energy-driven price pressures threaten medium-term price stability.\nBoJ board member Hajime Takata further bolstered hawkish expectations by advocating for a more nimble rate path, a day after U.S. Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent pressed Japanese officials at the G20 gathering in North Carolina for \"decisive\" action to address persistent yen weakness.\nRoushni Nair and Pranav Kashyap contributed to this article ","category_ids":[1],"country_ids":[4,5,6,25,43,72],"created_by":275864531,"domain_id":1,"economic_event_ids":null,"edited_by":0,"editors_pick":false,"featured":true,"id":4885309,"important":false,"instruments":[{"id":1,"primary_tag":false},{"id":3,"primary_tag":false},{"id":5,"primary_tag":false},{"id":8,"primary_tag":false},{"id":1538,"primary_tag":false},{"id":8827,"primary_tag":false},{"id":8833,"primary_tag":false},{"id":23705,"primary_tag":false},{"id":23878,"primary_tag":false}],"keywords":"","link":"/news/forex-news/asian-currencies-weaken-as-dollar-gains-kiwi-tumbles-on-rbnz-outlook-4885309","media":[{"caption":"","copyright":"\u0026copy; Reuters","purpose":"preview_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/LYNXMPEM4R1MU_150x108_S.jpg"},{"caption":"","copyright":"\u0026copy; Reuters","purpose":"medium_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/LYNXMPEM4R1MU_M.jpg"},{"caption":"","copyright":"\u0026copy; Reuters","purpose":"main_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/LYNXMPEM4R1MU_1200_L.jpg"},{"caption":"","copyright":"","purpose":"article_source_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/providers/investing-new.png"}],"metadata":{},"multi_publish_headers":null,"news_type":"AkapiAdmin","newsType":"AkapiAdmin","publish_company_id":null,"published_at":"2026-09-02T04:36:52Z","source_external_link":"","source_id":10,"source_name":"Investing.com","source_image":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/providers/investing-new.png","title":"Dollar lower as oil, yields lose steam; eyes on loonie, kiwi after rate decisions","updated_at":"2026-09-02T20:04:18Z","commentsCount":0,"category":"","categoryLink":"","provider":"Investing.com","date":"2026-09-02 20:04:18","writerId":0,"front_writer_name":"","front_member_name":"","front_writer_link":"","writerName":"","writerLink":"","writerImage":"","userFirstName":"","userLastName":"","writerArticleCount":0,"company_name":"","company_name_prepared":"","company_href":"","company_url":"","user_type":"","authorLink":"","editorId":0,"editorFirstName":"","editorLastName":"","editorLink":"","image_copyright":"\u0026copy; Reuters","related_image_big":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/LYNXMPEM4R1MU_1200_L.jpg","images_serialized":"a:1:{i:0;a:2:{s:3:\"src\";s:65:\"https://content-media.investing.com/news/LYNXMPEM4R1MU_1200_L.jpg\";s:5:\"width\";i:200;}}","imageHref":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/LYNXMPEM4R1MU_1200_L.jpg","sponsored":false},{"article_type":"news","automated_type":null,"body":"Investing.com -- Chevron said Wednesday it has reached updated terms with Venezuela for its joint ventures in the country and will invest more than $7 billion over the next five years, aiming to lift production to about 600,000 barrels per day. \nThe move follows months of separate negotiations from Washington’s recent announcement of a deal giving the U.S. majority control over roughly 65 billion barrels of Venezuelan oil reserves. Chevron said it had signed multiple agreements establishing new terms with its joint ventures to support future investment, project development and production growth, including additional acreage in the Orinoco Belt. \nThe agreements come after President Trump said last week that the U.S. had struck a deal with Venezuela to secure control of a large share of the country’s oil reserves. Venezuela’s oil sector deteriorated over years of mismanagement under Hugo Chavez and Nicolas Maduro, and since Maduro’s ouster in January, Trump has pushed American oil companies to increase investment there.\nChevron has kept operating in Venezuela for more than a century, while ExxonMobil and ConocoPhillips have stayed out of the country since exiting in 2007, when their assets were nationalized under Chavez.\nThe new investment will back production growth at Chevron’s three Venezuelan joint ventures, which have raised output by 15% this year. The company said total costs are expected to stay below $20 a barrel.\n\"Chevron’s history in Venezuela spans more than a century, and our expanded position reflects our confidence in the country’s deep resource potential and its ability to compete for investment within our portfolio for decades,\" Chevron CEO Mike Wirth said.\nThe energy giant has operated in Venezuela since 1923 through three joint ventures: Petroindependencia and Petropiar, both in the Orinoco Belt, and Petroboscan, in the western state of Zulia.","category_ids":[25],"country_ids":[5],"created_by":256370160,"domain_id":1,"economic_event_ids":null,"edited_by":0,"editors_pick":false,"featured":true,"id":4885823,"important":false,"instruments":[{"id":240,"primary_tag":true},{"id":8849,"primary_tag":true}],"keywords":"","link":"/news/stock-market-news/chevron-to-invest-7-billion-in-venezuela-after-new-joint-venture-deal-4885823","media":[{"caption":"","copyright":"\u0026copy; Reuters. ","purpose":"preview_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/LYNXMPED9Q177_S.jpg"},{"caption":"","copyright":"\u0026copy; Reuters. ","purpose":"medium_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/LYNXMPED9Q177_M.jpg"},{"caption":"","copyright":"\u0026copy; Reuters. ","purpose":"main_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/LYNXMPED9Q177_L.jpg"},{"caption":"","copyright":"","purpose":"article_source_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/providers/investing-new.png"}],"metadata":{},"multi_publish_headers":null,"news_type":"AkapiAdmin","newsType":"AkapiAdmin","publish_company_id":null,"published_at":"2026-09-02T11:01:19Z","source_external_link":"","source_id":10,"source_name":"Investing.com","source_image":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/providers/investing-new.png","title":"Chevron to invest $7 billion in Venezuela after new joint venture deal","updated_at":"2026-09-02T11:01:19Z","commentsCount":2,"category":"","categoryLink":"","provider":"Investing.com","date":"2026-09-02 11:01:19","writerId":0,"front_writer_name":"","front_member_name":"","front_writer_link":"","writerName":"","writerLink":"","writerImage":"","userFirstName":"","userLastName":"","writerArticleCount":0,"company_name":"","company_name_prepared":"","company_href":"","company_url":"","user_type":"","authorLink":"","editorId":0,"editorFirstName":"","editorLastName":"","editorLink":"","image_copyright":"\u0026copy; Reuters.","related_image_big":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/LYNXMPED9Q177_L.jpg","images_serialized":"a:1:{i:0;a:2:{s:3:\"src\";s:60:\"https://content-media.investing.com/news/LYNXMPED9Q177_L.jpg\";s:5:\"width\";i:200;}}","imageHref":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/LYNXMPED9Q177_L.jpg","sponsored":false},{"article_type":"news","automated_type":null,"body":"","category_ids":[461],"country_ids":[5],"created_by":278602837,"domain_id":1,"economic_event_ids":null,"edited_by":null,"editors_pick":false,"featured":true,"id":4884535,"important":true,"instruments":[{"id":252,"primary_tag":true}],"keywords":"","link":"/news/pro/bofa-thinks-this-tech-giants-ai-strategy-is-paying-off-432SI-4884535","media":[{"caption":"","copyright":"","purpose":"preview_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/Microsoft_2_150x108_S_1416303314.jpg"},{"caption":"","copyright":"","purpose":"medium_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/Microsoft_2_M_1440048702.jpg"},{"caption":"","copyright":"","purpose":"main_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/Microsoft_2_800x533_L_1412175603.jpg"},{"caption":"","copyright":"","purpose":"article_source_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/providers/investing-new.png"}],"metadata":{},"multi_publish_headers":null,"news_type":"InvestingPro","newsType":"InvestingPro","publish_company_id":null,"published_at":"2026-09-01T20:00:00Z","source_external_link":"https://www.streetinsider.com/","source_id":80,"source_name":"Investing.com","source_image":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/providers/investing-new.png","title":"BofA thinks this tech giant’s AI strategy is paying off","updated_at":"2026-09-01T20:01:02Z","commentsCount":0,"category":"","categoryLink":"","provider":"Investing.com","date":"2026-09-01 20:01:02","writerId":0,"front_writer_name":"","front_member_name":"","front_writer_link":"","writerName":"","writerLink":"","writerImage":"","userFirstName":"","userLastName":"","writerArticleCount":0,"company_name":"","company_name_prepared":"","company_href":"","company_url":"","user_type":"","authorLink":"","editorId":0,"editorFirstName":"","editorLastName":"","editorLink":"","image_copyright":"","related_image_big":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/Microsoft_2_800x533_L_1412175603.jpg","images_serialized":"a:1:{i:0;a:2:{s:3:\"src\";s:77:\"https://content-media.investing.com/news/Microsoft_2_800x533_L_1412175603.jpg\";s:5:\"width\";i:200;}}","imageHref":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/Microsoft_2_800x533_L_1412175603.jpg","sponsored":false},{"article_type":"news","automated_type":null,"body":"Investing.com -- Morgan Stanley said Apple’s (NASDAQ: AAPL) first foldable iPhone could contribute around $14 billion of December-quarter revenue, calling next week’s launch the company’s most consequential since the iPhone X.\n\"Apple’s first foldable iPhone is the biggest iPhone form-factor change since iPhone X,\" analyst Erik Woodring wrote, ahead of the Sept. 9 event. \nThe bank expects strong initial demand and constrained supply, with 7 million to 8 million builds in the second half of 2026 and up to 20 million over the first product cycle.\nMorgan Stanley framed the launch as a critical test of pricing power, expecting the largest like-for-like iPhone price increases in years. \nWoodring noted that rising NAND and DRAM costs are seen as driving Pro models up more than $200 year over year, with the key upgrade on the iPhone 18 Pro likely the move to TSMC’s 2nm process for higher on-device AI performance.\nThe bank said Apple appears \"more focused on securing enough components than finding enough buyers,\" though memory supply may cap near-term builds.\n“The event will prove to be anything but ordinary,” wrote Woodring. “This is because for the first time in 15 years, Tim Cook will not headline the event; instead, new CEO John Ternus will lead the company into its first major iPhone form factor change in nearly 10 years, unveiling the much-anticipated iPhone Fold (Ultra?), alongside what are likely to be the broadest, and most significant, like-for-like iPhone price hikes in company history.”\nThe bank does not expect the base iPhone 18 or iPhone Air 2 until spring.\n ","category_ids":[25],"country_ids":[5],"created_by":245462300,"domain_id":1,"economic_event_ids":null,"edited_by":0,"editors_pick":false,"featured":true,"id":4886148,"important":false,"instruments":[{"id":6408,"primary_tag":true}],"keywords":"","link":"/news/stock-market-news/could-apples-foldable-iphone-be-a-14b-opportunity-morgan-stanley-weighs-in-4886148","media":[{"caption":"","copyright":"\u0026copy; Reuters","purpose":"preview_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/moved_small-LYNXMPEM7D146_L.jpg"},{"caption":"","copyright":"\u0026copy; Reuters","purpose":"main_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/moved_LYNXMPEM7D146_L.jpg"},{"caption":"","copyright":"","purpose":"article_source_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/providers/investing-new.png"}],"metadata":{},"multi_publish_headers":null,"news_type":"AkapiAdmin","newsType":"AkapiAdmin","publish_company_id":null,"published_at":"2026-09-02T12:45:00Z","source_external_link":"","source_id":10,"source_name":"Investing.com","source_image":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/providers/investing-new.png","title":"Could Apple’s foldable iPhone be a $14B opportunity? Morgan Stanley weighs in","updated_at":"2026-09-02T12:45:00Z","commentsCount":3,"category":"","categoryLink":"","provider":"Investing.com","date":"2026-09-02 12:45:00","writerId":0,"front_writer_name":"","front_member_name":"","front_writer_link":"","writerName":"","writerLink":"","writerImage":"","userFirstName":"","userLastName":"","writerArticleCount":0,"company_name":"","company_name_prepared":"","company_href":"","company_url":"","user_type":"","authorLink":"","editorId":0,"editorFirstName":"","editorLastName":"","editorLink":"","image_copyright":"\u0026copy; Reuters","related_image_big":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/moved_LYNXMPEM7D146_L.jpg","images_serialized":"a:1:{i:0;a:2:{s:3:\"src\";s:66:\"https://content-media.investing.com/news/moved_LYNXMPEM7D146_L.jpg\";s:5:\"width\";i:200;}}","imageHref":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/moved_LYNXMPEM7D146_L.jpg","sponsored":false},{"article_type":"news","automated_type":null,"body":"Investing.com -- Symbiotic, the collateral markets platform backed by Paradigm, Pantera Capital and Coinbase Ventures, has appointed former Société Générale Asset Management executive Mahendran Nathan as its global head of onchain solutions, Investing.com has learned.\nNathan, who will be based in Singapore, took up the role in August. He joins as the company steps up efforts to draw institutional capital into onchain markets, a shift that depends on giving pension funds, insurers and sovereign wealth funds the investment structures, governance and reporting they need to take part.\nAt Société Générale Asset Management, Nathan was chief executive and regional head for Asia Pacific, with Symbiotic noting that he scaled the platform from $300 million to $15 billion in assets under management over seven years and won mandates from sovereign wealth funds, central banks, pension funds and insurers. \nHe later served as CEO for ASEAN, Hong Kong, Taiwan and India business at Russell Investments, and since 2022 has worked as an allocator across public and private markets.\nAt Symbiotic, he will lead the design of investment products for asset owners and allocators, alongside capital formation and institutional coverage across the U.S., EMEA and APAC.\nMisha Putiatin, co-founder of Symbiotic, said Nathan’s \"experience brings us closer to making institutional-scale onchain markets real.\"\n ","category_ids":[301],"country_ids":null,"created_by":245462300,"domain_id":1,"economic_event_ids":null,"edited_by":0,"editors_pick":false,"featured":true,"id":4885912,"important":false,"instruments":null,"keywords":"","link":"/news/cryptocurrency-news/exclusive-symbiotic-hires-exsocgen-am-apac-chief-to-lead-onchain-push-4885912","media":[{"caption":"","copyright":"","purpose":"preview_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/two-hands-shaking-(agreement)2_150x108_S_1416304100.jpg"},{"caption":"","copyright":"","purpose":"medium_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/two-hands-shaking-(agreement)2_M_1440049623.jpg"},{"caption":"","copyright":"","purpose":"main_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/two-hands-shaking-(agreement)2_800x533_L_1412520354.jpg"},{"caption":"","copyright":"","purpose":"article_source_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/providers/investing-new.png"}],"metadata":{},"multi_publish_headers":null,"news_type":"AkapiAdmin","newsType":"AkapiAdmin","publish_company_id":null,"published_at":"2026-09-02T11:30:53Z","source_external_link":"","source_id":10,"source_name":"Investing.com","source_image":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/providers/investing-new.png","title":"Exclusive: Symbiotic hires ex-SocGen AM APAC chief to lead onchain push","updated_at":"2026-09-02T11:30:53Z","commentsCount":0,"category":"","categoryLink":"","provider":"Investing.com","date":"2026-09-02 11:30:53","writerId":0,"front_writer_name":"","front_member_name":"","front_writer_link":"","writerName":"","writerLink":"","writerImage":"","userFirstName":"","userLastName":"","writerArticleCount":0,"company_name":"","company_name_prepared":"","company_href":"","company_url":"","user_type":"","authorLink":"","editorId":0,"editorFirstName":"","editorLastName":"","editorLink":"","image_copyright":"","related_image_big":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/two-hands-shaking-(agreement)2_800x533_L_1412520354.jpg","images_serialized":"a:1:{i:0;a:2:{s:3:\"src\";s:96:\"https://content-media.investing.com/news/two-hands-shaking-(agreement)2_800x533_L_1412520354.jpg\";s:5:\"width\";i:200;}}","imageHref":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/two-hands-shaking-(agreement)2_800x533_L_1412520354.jpg","sponsored":false},{"article_type":"news","automated_type":null,"body":"Investing.com-- Bitcoin edged lower on Wednesday, extending losses after logging strong monthly gains in August as renewed U.S.-Iran fighting and persistent uncertainty over interest rates weighed on risk-driven assets. \nThe world’s biggest crypto fell 0.9% to $77,387.6 by 09:46 ET (13:46 GMT), after rallying nearly 25% in August.\nBut this rally came to a halt in September as crypto markets were spooked by a resurgence in Treasury yields, while renewed U.S.-Iran military action also dented risk appetite.\nRenewed buying action by top corporate holder Strategy offered limited support to Bitcoin, even as the company marked its first Bitcoin purchase in two months. \n\nU.S.-Iran tensions, rate jitters stall Bitcoin rally \nThe U.S. and Iran traded a fresh round of strikes overnight on Tuesday, as the countries remained at odds over the Strait of Hormuz. \nBoth sides signaled little intent to deescalate, with U.S. President Donald Trump threatening to attack Iran’s oil infrastructure, while Tehran warned of more attacks on U.S. bases in surrounding Gulf countries. \nOil prices rose sharply on the renewed fighting, which marked the worst U.S.-Iran hostilities in over a month. \nHigher oil in turn factored into concerns over surging energy-driven inflation across the globe. Government bond yields across Japan, Australia, the U.S. and Europe surged on this notion, further pressuring risk-driven assets.\nMarkets were seen rapidly increasing bets that the Federal Reserve will hike interest rates in September, especially as inflation remains well above the bank’s 2% annual target.\nHigher rates bode poorly for purely speculative assets such as Bitcoin. The crypto’s August rally was fueled chiefly by a drop in yields. \nFocus this week is squarely on U.S. nonfarm payrolls data, due Friday, for more cues on rates. Any signs of resilience in the labor market give the Fed more headroom to hike. \nCrypto price today: altcoins extend losses after strong August \nBroader crypto prices largely retreated on Wednesday after also logging strong gains in August.\nWorld no.2 crypto Ether fell 1.4% to $2,408.3, while XRP slid 2.5% to $1.3406. \nSolana and Cardano fell 2.6% and 1.2%, respectively, while BNB traded flat. \nMemecoins Dogecoin and $TRUMP fell 1.3% and 6.2%, respectively. \nVahid Karaahmetovic contributed to this report. ","category_ids":[301],"country_ids":[4,5,999],"created_by":240569828,"domain_id":1,"economic_event_ids":[227],"edited_by":0,"editors_pick":false,"featured":true,"id":4885342,"important":false,"instruments":[{"id":8833,"primary_tag":false},{"id":8849,"primary_tag":false},{"id":16678,"primary_tag":false},{"id":945629,"primary_tag":true},{"id":1054919,"primary_tag":false},{"id":1058142,"primary_tag":false},{"id":1073899,"primary_tag":false},{"id":1118146,"primary_tag":false},{"id":1158819,"primary_tag":false},{"id":1173409,"primary_tag":false},{"id":1187654,"primary_tag":true},{"id":1211376,"primary_tag":false}],"keywords":"","link":"/news/cryptocurrency-news/bitcoin-drops-to-776k-as-usiran-fighting-rate-jitters-weigh-4885342","media":[{"caption":"","copyright":"\u0026copy; Reuters","purpose":"preview_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/moved_small-LYNXMPEHAF0OC_L.jpg"},{"caption":"","copyright":"\u0026copy; Reuters","purpose":"main_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/moved_LYNXMPEHAF0OC_L.jpg"},{"caption":"","copyright":"","purpose":"article_source_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/providers/investing-new.png"}],"metadata":{},"multi_publish_headers":null,"news_type":"AkapiAdmin","newsType":"AkapiAdmin","publish_company_id":null,"published_at":"2026-09-02T06:20:06Z","source_external_link":"","source_id":10,"source_name":"Investing.com","source_image":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/providers/investing-new.png","title":"Bitcoin dips to $77.3k as U.S.-Iran fighting, rate jitters weigh","updated_at":"2026-09-02T13:48:48Z","commentsCount":1,"category":"","categoryLink":"","provider":"Investing.com","date":"2026-09-02 13:48:48","writerId":0,"front_writer_name":"","front_member_name":"","front_writer_link":"","writerName":"","writerLink":"","writerImage":"","userFirstName":"","userLastName":"","writerArticleCount":0,"company_name":"","company_name_prepared":"","company_href":"","company_url":"","user_type":"","authorLink":"","editorId":0,"editorFirstName":"","editorLastName":"","editorLink":"","image_copyright":"\u0026copy; Reuters","related_image_big":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/moved_LYNXMPEHAF0OC_L.jpg","images_serialized":"a:1:{i:0;a:2:{s:3:\"src\";s:66:\"https://content-media.investing.com/news/moved_LYNXMPEHAF0OC_L.jpg\";s:5:\"width\";i:200;}}","imageHref":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/moved_LYNXMPEHAF0OC_L.jpg","sponsored":false},{"article_type":"news","automated_type":null,"body":"","category_ids":[461],"country_ids":[21],"created_by":245462300,"domain_id":1,"economic_event_ids":null,"edited_by":null,"editors_pick":false,"featured":true,"id":4884034,"important":true,"instruments":[{"id":6917,"primary_tag":true}],"keywords":"","link":"/news/pro/why-ubs-thinks-this-european-chipmaker-is-still-undervalued-despite-its-premium-valuation-432SI-4884034","media":[{"caption":"","copyright":"\u0026copy; Investing.com","purpose":"preview_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/1200_6_150x108_S_1783516494.jpg"},{"caption":"","copyright":"\u0026copy; Investing.com","purpose":"medium_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/1200_6_800x533_L_1783516494.jpg"},{"caption":"","copyright":"\u0026copy; Investing.com","purpose":"main_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/1200_6_800x533_L_1783516494.jpg"},{"caption":"","copyright":"","purpose":"article_source_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/providers/investing-new.png"}],"metadata":{},"multi_publish_headers":null,"news_type":"InvestingPro","newsType":"InvestingPro","publish_company_id":null,"published_at":"2026-09-01T12:33:01Z","source_external_link":"https://www.streetinsider.com/","source_id":80,"source_name":"Investing.com","source_image":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/providers/investing-new.png","title":"Why UBS thinks this European chipmaker is still undervalued despite its premium valuation","updated_at":"2026-09-01T12:35:02Z","commentsCount":1,"category":"","categoryLink":"","provider":"Investing.com","date":"2026-09-01 12:35:02","writerId":0,"front_writer_name":"","front_member_name":"","front_writer_link":"","writerName":"","writerLink":"","writerImage":"","userFirstName":"","userLastName":"","writerArticleCount":0,"company_name":"","company_name_prepared":"","company_href":"","company_url":"","user_type":"","authorLink":"","editorId":0,"editorFirstName":"","editorLastName":"","editorLink":"","image_copyright":"\u0026copy; Investing.com","related_image_big":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/1200_6_800x533_L_1783516494.jpg","images_serialized":"a:1:{i:0;a:2:{s:3:\"src\";s:72:\"https://content-media.investing.com/news/1200_6_800x533_L_1783516494.jpg\";s:5:\"width\";i:200;}}","imageHref":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/1200_6_800x533_L_1783516494.jpg","sponsored":false},{"article_type":"news","automated_type":null,"body":"Investing.com -  A relentless global sovereign bond selloff extended into its sixth consecutive session on Wednesday, driving borrowing costs across Europe, Asia, and North America to multi-year and multi-decade peaks as a toxic cocktail of military escalation in the Middle East, corporate debt issuance for artificial intelligence, and persistent central bank hawkishness sparked a historic retreat from fixed-income paper. \nA relentless global sovereign bond selloff extended into its sixth consecutive session on Wednesday, driving borrowing costs across North America, Europe, and Asia to multi-year and multi-decade peaks as a toxic combination of military escalation in the Middle East, corporate debt issuance for artificial intelligence, and persistent central bank hawkishness sparked a historic retreat from fixed-income paper.\nIn the United States, benchmark Treasury yields reached fresh highs across the curve as fixed-income desks aggressively re-priced long-term interest rate risks:\n\n\n10-Year Treasury Yield: Advanced to 4.787%, its highest level since 2025, steadily inching toward the psychologically crucial 5.0% threshold.\n\n\nTwo-Year Treasury Yield: Hovered at 4.388%, also reaching its highest level since 2025 as policy-sensitive short-end paper reflected hawkish central bank expectations.\n\n\n30-Year Treasury Yield: Advanced to 5.259%, touching a near two-week high as long-end duration paper faced continued supply and term-premia pressure.\n\n\nThe 5% level represents an aggressive hurdle rate for capital allocation: when risk-free sovereign debt offers guaranteed 5% annual yields, the relative value proposition of risk assets- especially equities with high price-to-earnings valuations - collapses.\nFurthermore, because the 10-year yield serves as the global baseline for corporate debt financing, consumer credit cards, commercial loans, and 30-year fixed mortgages, crossing 5% triggers an immediate tightening of broader financial conditions. High long-term borrowing costs threaten to slow economic growth, compress corporate profit margins, and increase debt-refinancing risks across highly leveraged balance sheets.\n \n\n\nIn Europe, Germany’s policy-sensitive two-year Schatz yield rose for a sixth straight day to hit 2.983%, its highest level since 2024.\nLong-end core yields suffered an even sharper drop in price, with the benchmark German 10-year Bund yield climbing to a 2011 high of 3.370% and the 30-year yield advancing to 3.845%, also its highest since 2011.\nFrance’s 10-year OAT yield escalated to 4.244%, reaching levels not seen since the height of the 2008 global financial crisis as mounting fiscal deficits amplified broader rate pressures across the single-currency bloc.\n\"When traditional borrowing costs climb like this, people holding appreciating assets start asking a simple question,\" said Himanshu Sahay, Arch Lending’s co-Founder and chief revenue officer.\n\"Why sell, or take on more expensive debt, when the asset itself can be collateral? It’s not about timing markets, it’s about not wanting to give up long-term upside just to cover a short-term cash need.\"\nAussie yields surge on GDP Beat; JGBs anchor generations-high shift\nThe fixed-income rout extended deep into the Asia-Pacific region, where Australia’s benchmark 10-year government bond yield jumped to 5.205%, its highest since 2011.\nThe Australian bond selloff gathered momentum after resilient domestic gross domestic product figures and a unexpectedly hot July inflation readout reinforced expectations that the Reserve Bank of Australia will deliver a fourth interest rate hike before year-end.\nMeanwhile, Japan’s 10-year government bond yield hovered above 3.000%, anchoring at its highest point since 1996 in a historic transformation for a debt market that for decades boasted the lowest sovereign yields on the planet under the Bank of Japan’s ultra-loose regime.\nTokyo now faces intense public and private pressure from Washington to aggressively tighten monetary settings, with U.S. officials arguing that higher Japanese yields are necessary to stabilize the yen and curb capital outflows.\nHormuz standoff and Oil spike invert safe-haven playbook\nThe global wave of bond selling reflects a fundamental breakdown in traditional asset dynamics. Rather than serving as a shock absorber or safe haven during geopolitical crises, sovereign paper is being aggressively dumped by institutional allocators as direct U.S.-Iranian military exchanges threaten a prolonged energy-driven inflation shock.\nMarkets remained on high alert after Washington conducted another round of air strikes against Iran’s Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps - marking its second direct attack on Iranian targets this week - following Iranian missile retaliations against U.S. air bases in Jordan.\nThe two nations remain locked in a tense standoff over the Strait of Hormuz, where despite U.S. assurances that the waterway remains open to commercial shipping, maritime tracking data reveals that vessel traffic is running at a mere fraction of pre-war levels.\nWith U.S. President Donald Trump warning of significantly harder strikes if Tehran retaliates - and openly threatening targeted actions against Kharg Island, Iran’s primary crude export hub - global energy benchmarks have soared past $90 a barrel.\nThe prospect of an enduring blockade along one of the world’s most critical oil transit arteries threatens to pass directly into transportation and consumer fuel costs, reigniting cost-push inflation and severely undercutting the ability of central banks like the Federal Reserve to hold interest rates steady, let alone cut them.\nCorporate AI debt tsunami floods capital markets\nCompounding the macro headwinds generated by the Middle East energy shock, global debt markets are also buckling under an unprecedented tsunami of corporate bond supply.\nTechnology heavyweights and multinational conglomerates are tapping debt capital markets at a record-breaking pace to finance capital expenditure for artificial intelligence infrastructure, data center construction, and advanced semiconductor procurement.\nThis historic wave of high-grade corporate debt issuance is competing directly with sovereign Treasuries, Bunds, and JGBs for institutional capital at a time when major central banks are actively unwinding their balance sheets through quantitative tightening.\nWith sovereign and corporate issuers flooding the market simultaneously, global capital desks are demanding drastically higher term premia to absorb the glut, ensuring that sovereign yields continue their march into territory not seen in decades.\n\n","category_ids":[1],"country_ids":[4,5,17,22,25,35],"created_by":280876503,"domain_id":1,"economic_event_ids":null,"edited_by":0,"editors_pick":false,"featured":true,"id":4885454,"important":false,"instruments":[{"id":8833,"primary_tag":false},{"id":8986,"primary_tag":false},{"id":23685,"primary_tag":false},{"id":23693,"primary_tag":false},{"id":23696,"primary_tag":false},{"id":23701,"primary_tag":false},{"id":23705,"primary_tag":false},{"id":23706,"primary_tag":false},{"id":23778,"primary_tag":false},{"id":23878,"primary_tag":false},{"id":23901,"primary_tag":false},{"id":1081628,"primary_tag":false}],"keywords":"","link":"/news/forex-news/sovereign-debt-plummets--as-inflation-fears-push-yields-to-multiyear-peaks-4885454","media":[{"caption":"","copyright":"","purpose":"preview_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/JapaneseYen_150x108_S_1411905912.jpg"},{"caption":"","copyright":"","purpose":"medium_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/JapaneseYen_M_1440047753.jpg"},{"caption":"","copyright":"","purpose":"main_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/JapaneseYen_800x533_L_1411905912.jpg"},{"caption":"","copyright":"","purpose":"article_source_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/providers/investing-new.png"}],"metadata":{},"multi_publish_headers":null,"news_type":"AkapiAdmin","newsType":"AkapiAdmin","publish_company_id":null,"published_at":"2026-09-02T08:01:03Z","source_external_link":"","source_id":10,"source_name":"Investing.com","source_image":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/providers/investing-new.png","title":"Sovereign debt plummets as inflation fears push yields to multi-year peaks","updated_at":"2026-09-02T13:07:35Z","commentsCount":5,"category":"","categoryLink":"","provider":"Investing.com","date":"2026-09-02 13:07:35","writerId":0,"front_writer_name":"","front_member_name":"","front_writer_link":"","writerName":"","writerLink":"","writerImage":"","userFirstName":"","userLastName":"","writerArticleCount":0,"company_name":"","company_name_prepared":"","company_href":"","company_url":"","user_type":"","authorLink":"","editorId":0,"editorFirstName":"","editorLastName":"","editorLink":"","image_copyright":"","related_image_big":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/JapaneseYen_800x533_L_1411905912.jpg","images_serialized":"a:1:{i:0;a:2:{s:3:\"src\";s:77:\"https://content-media.investing.com/news/JapaneseYen_800x533_L_1411905912.jpg\";s:5:\"width\";i:200;}}","imageHref":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/JapaneseYen_800x533_L_1411905912.jpg","sponsored":false},{"article_type":"news","automated_type":null,"body":"Investing.com -- Federal Reserve Bank of New York President John Williams said Tuesday that rising long-term bond yields reflect a strong economy rather than inflation concerns, as he continues to gather data ahead of the next monetary policy decision.\nWilliams told CNBC that increases in borrowing costs are \"really a strong U.S. economy and a strong economic outlook fueled by big investments in AI and data centers and technology in general.\"\n\nThe Fed official noted a correlation between bond yields and the Middle East conflict. He said tariffs and the Middle East war are major drivers of inflation remaining above the central bank’s target.\nWilliams stated that inflation expectations remain contained and the Fed is not seeing second-round inflation impacts from tariffs. He described recent inflation data as encouraging and said the trend points toward lower inflation.\nThe labor market remains stable and solid, Williams said, adding that achieving 2% inflation in the foreseeable future is the Fed’s primary objective.\nWilliams expressed optimism about the long-term economic impact of artificial intelligence. He said strong investment demand is putting upward pressure on yields.\nThe New York Fed president said he supported the outcome of the July Federal Open Market Committee meeting and needs to collect more data before the next FOMC meeting. He added that monetary policy implementation is working well and Treasury debt management patterns do not complicate matters for the Fed.","category_ids":[14],"country_ids":[5],"created_by":235660125,"domain_id":1,"economic_event_ids":null,"edited_by":0,"editors_pick":false,"featured":true,"id":4886156,"important":false,"instruments":[{"id":23705,"primary_tag":true}],"keywords":"","link":"/news/economy-news/feds-williams-says-bond-yields-rise-amid-strong-economy-not-inflation-4886156","media":[{"caption":"","copyright":"\u0026copy; Reuters","purpose":"preview_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/moved_small-LYNXNPEL230QN_L.jpg"},{"caption":"","copyright":"\u0026copy; Reuters","purpose":"main_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/moved_LYNXNPEL230QN_L.jpg"},{"caption":"","copyright":"","purpose":"article_source_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/providers/investing-new.png"}],"metadata":{},"multi_publish_headers":null,"news_type":"AkapiAdmin","newsType":"AkapiAdmin","publish_company_id":null,"published_at":"2026-09-02T12:51:13Z","source_external_link":"","source_id":10,"source_name":"Investing.com","source_image":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/providers/investing-new.png","title":"Fed’s Williams says bond yields rise amid strong economy, not inflation","updated_at":"2026-09-02T12:51:13Z","commentsCount":5,"category":"","categoryLink":"","provider":"Investing.com","date":"2026-09-02 12:51:13","writerId":0,"front_writer_name":"","front_member_name":"","front_writer_link":"","writerName":"","writerLink":"","writerImage":"","userFirstName":"","userLastName":"","writerArticleCount":0,"company_name":"","company_name_prepared":"","company_href":"","company_url":"","user_type":"","authorLink":"","editorId":0,"editorFirstName":"","editorLastName":"","editorLink":"","image_copyright":"\u0026copy; Reuters","related_image_big":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/moved_LYNXNPEL230QN_L.jpg","images_serialized":"a:1:{i:0;a:2:{s:3:\"src\";s:66:\"https://content-media.investing.com/news/moved_LYNXNPEL230QN_L.jpg\";s:5:\"width\";i:200;}}","imageHref":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/moved_LYNXNPEL230QN_L.jpg","sponsored":false},{"article_type":"news","automated_type":null,"body":"Investing.com -- ING expects the European Central Bank (ECB) to raise interest rates by 25 basis points at next week’s meeting, marking the second hike this year as policymakers weigh persistent energy-driven inflation against signs of economic resilience.\nCarsten Brzeski, global head of macro research at ING, said the case for a hike has strengthened since July, when some ECB members had already advocated for one. Since then, the eurozone economy has shown \"an almost unexpected resilience\" to the war in the Middle East, partly aided by Asian competitors losing orders to European rivals following the closure of the Strait of Hormuz, as well as long-planned fiscal stimulus, Brzeski said. \nHeadline inflation has continued climbing and is expected to stay above 3% year-on-year for the rest of the year, even as core and services inflation \"currently provide no reason to panic.\" With oil prices elevated and the risk of a fresh gas price shock rising, Brzeski said it would be difficult for most ECB policymakers not to see a case for another hike.\nThe new round of ECB staff projections is not expected to bring major changes, though Brzeski said growth and inflation forecasts should be revised slightly higher, largely due to earlier upward revisions to first-quarter growth and higher oil prices.\nWhether the ECB extends its hiking cycle beyond September remains less certain. Markets have started pricing in at least one more hike before year-end, and views within the ECB appear to be diverging, with officials including Isabel Schnabel and Irish central bank governor Gabriel Makhlouf signaling willingness to tighten further.\nStill, Brzeski noted that after next week’s hike, the deposit rate at 2.5% would remain within the range the ECB itself considers neutral. Going further, he said, would suggest the ECB views restrictive policy as necessary.\n\"But there is a big difference between an economy that has shown resilience and an overheating economy that needs restrictive monetary policy,\" he added. \"We still find it hard to see – amid public finance woes and surging bond yields – that the ECB would really be willing to add more fuel to the fire. Or in other words, it’s difficult to envisage the ECB being willing to risk a recession to tackle what is still a textbook supply-side shock.\"\nThe recent surge in bond yields is also expected to feature in next week’s discussion. Brzeski said rising yields are, in some ways, doing the ECB’s job by tightening financing conditions, but warned that excessive or uneven tightening across eurozone countries could create new problems.\nLooking further ahead, he flagged growing attention to debt sustainability concerns, particularly ahead of next year’s French presidential election, along with early speculation about a potential successor to ECB President Christine Lagarde.","category_ids":[14],"country_ids":[17,72],"created_by":256370160,"domain_id":1,"economic_event_ids":null,"edited_by":0,"editors_pick":false,"featured":true,"id":4885652,"important":false,"instruments":[{"id":1,"primary_tag":true},{"id":23693,"primary_tag":true},{"id":40823,"primary_tag":true}],"keywords":"","link":"/news/economy-news/can-the-ecb-hike-rates-without-triggering-more-market-turmoil-4885652","media":[{"caption":"","copyright":"\u0026copy; Reuters","purpose":"preview_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/moved_small-LYNXMPEIAF0HP_L.jpg"},{"caption":"","copyright":"\u0026copy; Reuters","purpose":"main_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/moved_LYNXMPEIAF0HP_L.jpg"},{"caption":"","copyright":"","purpose":"article_source_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/providers/investing-new.png"}],"metadata":{},"multi_publish_headers":null,"news_type":"AkapiAdmin","newsType":"AkapiAdmin","publish_company_id":null,"published_at":"2026-09-02T09:56:39Z","source_external_link":"","source_id":10,"source_name":"Investing.com","source_image":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/providers/investing-new.png","title":"Can the ECB hike rates without triggering more market turmoil?","updated_at":"2026-09-02T09:56:39Z","commentsCount":2,"category":"","categoryLink":"","provider":"Investing.com","date":"2026-09-02 09:56:39","writerId":0,"front_writer_name":"","front_member_name":"","front_writer_link":"","writerName":"","writerLink":"","writerImage":"","userFirstName":"","userLastName":"","writerArticleCount":0,"company_name":"","company_name_prepared":"","company_href":"","company_url":"","user_type":"","authorLink":"","editorId":0,"editorFirstName":"","editorLastName":"","editorLink":"","image_copyright":"\u0026copy; Reuters","related_image_big":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/moved_LYNXMPEIAF0HP_L.jpg","images_serialized":"a:1:{i:0;a:2:{s:3:\"src\";s:66:\"https://content-media.investing.com/news/moved_LYNXMPEIAF0HP_L.jpg\";s:5:\"width\";i:200;}}","imageHref":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/moved_LYNXMPEIAF0HP_L.jpg","sponsored":false},{"article_type":"news","automated_type":"triggers","body":"CAMPBELL, Calif. - ChargePoint Holdings, Inc. (NYSE:CHPT) reported second quarter fiscal 2027 results that exceeded analyst expectations, sending shares up 16.5% as the electric vehicle charging company demonstrated improved operational discipline and stronger-than-expected revenue growth.\nThe company posted a loss of -$1.35 per share for the quarter ended July 31, 2026, beating the analyst consensus estimate of -$1.60. Revenue reached $116 million, surpassing the $105.43 million analyst estimate and marking an 18% increase from $98.6 million in the prior year’s same quarter. Networked charging systems revenue grew 25% YoY to $62.9 million, while subscription revenue increased 10% YoY to $44 million.\n\"The second quarter was an exceptional quarter for ChargePoint as we exceeded the high end of our guidance, delivered record non-GAAP gross margin, and managed our cash with extreme rigor through continued operational discipline,\" said Rick Wilmer, President and Chief Executive Officer of ChargePoint.\nThe company reported adjusted gross margin of 38%, up from 33% in the prior year’s quarter, with a 4 percentage point benefit from tariff refunds. Adjusted EBITDA loss improved significantly to $4.8 million from a loss of $22.1 million YoY, representing a 78% improvement.\nFor the third quarter of fiscal 2027 ending October 31, 2026, ChargePoint provided revenue guidance of $105 million to $115 million. The midpoint of $110 million compares to the prior year’s third quarter revenue, though no analyst consensus for the upcoming quarter was provided.\nThis article was generated with the support of AI and reviewed by an editor. For more information see our T\u0026C.","category_ids":[1062],"country_ids":[5],"created_by":-1,"domain_id":1,"economic_event_ids":null,"edited_by":236550687,"editors_pick":false,"featured":true,"id":4886725,"important":true,"instruments":[{"id":1163535,"primary_tag":true}],"keywords":"","link":"/news/earnings/chargepoint-shares-soar-16-after-topping-estimates-as-revenue-surges-18-yoy-93CH-4886725","media":[{"caption":"","copyright":"\u0026copy; Reuters. ","purpose":"preview_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/LYNXNPEC0Q1B5_S.jpg"},{"caption":"","copyright":"\u0026copy; Reuters. ","purpose":"medium_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/LYNXNPEC0Q1B5_M.jpg"},{"caption":"","copyright":"\u0026copy; Reuters. ","purpose":"main_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/LYNXNPEC0Q1B5_L.jpg"},{"caption":"","copyright":"","purpose":"article_source_image","url":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/providers/investing-new.png"}],"metadata":{},"multi_publish_headers":null,"news_type":"Investing.com AI","newsType":"Investing.com AI","publish_company_id":null,"published_at":"2026-09-02T20:20:05Z","source_external_link":"","source_id":93,"source_name":"Investing.com","source_image":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/providers/investing-new.png","title":"ChargePoint shares soar 16% after topping estimates as revenue surges 18% YoY","updated_at":"2026-09-02T20:20:05Z","commentsCount":0,"category":"","categoryLink":"","provider":"Investing.com","date":"2026-09-02 20:20:05","writerId":0,"front_writer_name":"","front_member_name":"","front_writer_link":"","writerName":"","writerLink":"","writerImage":"","userFirstName":"","userLastName":"","writerArticleCount":0,"company_name":"","company_name_prepared":"","company_href":"","company_url":"","user_type":"","authorLink":"","editorId":0,"editorFirstName":"","editorLastName":"","editorLink":"","image_copyright":"\u0026copy; Reuters.","related_image_big":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/LYNXNPEC0Q1B5_L.jpg","images_serialized":"a:1:{i:0;a:2:{s:3:\"src\";s:60:\"https://content-media.investing.com/news/LYNXNPEC0Q1B5_L.jpg\";s:5:\"width\";i:200;}}","imageHref":"https://content-media.investing.com/news/LYNXNPEC0Q1B5_L.jpg","sponsored":false},{"article_type":"news","automated_type":null,"body":" By Juby Babu Sept 2 (Reuters) - Hewlett Packard Enterprise raised its annual forecasts on Wednesday after reporting quarterly results that beat Wall Street expectations, as surging AI-related demand across networking and servers drove growth across its business. HPE’s high-powered servers, which are essential for building and training AI models, have been in high demand from cloud computing giants and large enterprises. Enterprise IT infrastructure spending is strengthening, driven by continued investment in AI, server refresh cycles and rising demand for data center capacity, Citi analysts have said. \"It’s clear to us that the market, the tailwinds in AI, particularly the adoption of AI in the enterprise, is really starting to settle in,\" CFO Marie Myers told Reuters in an interview. HPE expects enterprise adoption of AI to support growth beyond the current fiscal year, Myers said, after customers spent fiscal 2025 and 2026 testing AI systems and beginning deployments that are showing productivity gains. HPE on Wednesday also announced an expansion to its collaboration with Oracle to help scale the software giant’s global AI infrastructure, deploying HPE Juniper Networking equipment across Oracle’s AI data centers. Myers noted that supply remained constrained, with memory being the main bottleneck, followed by NAND, CPUs and drives, though HPE has signed longer-term supply agreements to improve access to components. \"Demand is far outstripping supply.\" HPE forecast fiscal 2026 revenue growth of 34% to 37%, up from 29% to 33% previously, and raised its adjusted earnings forecast to between $3.75 and $3.85 per share, from $3.35 and $3.45. For fiscal 2027, HPE now projects revenue growth of 13% to 17%, up from 8% to 12%, with adjusted EPS growth of 16% to 20%, versus 12% to 16% forecast previously. \nThird-quarter revenue grew 33.6% to $12.21 billion, beating estimates of $11.91 billion. HPE earned $1.11 per share on an adjusted basis, ahead of estimates of 93 cents. HPE’s results follow strong forecasts from rivals Dell and Super Micro as Big Tech’s AI spending is set to exceed $730 billion this year. ","category_ids":[25],"country_ids":[5],"created_by":-1,"domain_id":1,"economic_event_ids":null,"edited_by":null,"editors_pick":false,"featured":true,"id":4886707,"important":false,"instruments":[{"id":959998,"primary_tag":true}],"keywords":"","link":"/news/stock-market-news/hpe-raises-annual-forecasts-as-ai-networking-demand-lifts-quarterly-revenue-4886707","media":